      "id": 1,
      "text": "Lucedale was founded in 1901 and named after lumber businessman Gregory Marston Luce.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucedale%2C_Mississippi"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Lucedale was one of several settlements created after the Mobile, Jackson and Kansas City Railroad penetrated northern Jackson County (now George County) in the late 1890s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucedale%2C_Mississippi"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Lucedale Public Library is part of the Jackson-George Regional Library System, serving the residents of George County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucedale%2C_Mississippi"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Lucedale serves as the county seat of George County, Mississippi, making it the administrative hub for the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucedale%2C_Mississippi"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "George Regional Hospital in Lucedale is a 50-bed, non-profit, community-owned hospital serving George County, Greene County, Stone County, and northern Jackson County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
